              509.06500244140625,
              103.62728881835938
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "7.1",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              117.86051177978516,
              88.2750015258789,
              130.88050842285156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Introduction ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              100.80000305175781,
              117.86051177978516,
              168.3350067138672,
              130.88050842285156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weather is not a capricious act of nature but, rather, the atmosphere’s response to unequal rates of ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              138.60244750976562,
              542.6824951171875,
              149.5824432373047
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "radiational heating and cooling across the surface of the Earth and within its atmosphere. The absorption of ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              151.20242309570312,
              542.5914306640625,
              162.1824188232422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "incoming solar radiation causes heating, while the emission of outgoing terrestrial radiation causes cooling. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              163.86245727539062,
              542.5474853515625,
              174.8424530029297
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "However, imbalances in the rate of heating and cooling create temperature gradients.",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              176.52243041992188,
              474.2770690917969,
              187.50242614746094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "circulations and weather are the atmosphere’s never-ending attempt to redistribute this heat and achieve ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              189.18246459960938,
              542.6167602539062,
              200.16246032714844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "equilibrium. This chapter provides a background on the interaction between the Earth and the atmosphere ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              201.84243774414062,
              542.46826171875,
              212.8224334716797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "for a better understanding of the weather that will affect flight operations. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              214.50247192382812,
              398.54498291015625,
              225.4824676513672
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "2",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 6.480000019073486,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              709.204345703125,
              75.23999786376953,
              715.6843872070312
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A gradient describes the rate of change of a feature (in this case, temperature) per unit of distance. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              75.23999786376953,
              709.8351440429688,
              472.5249938964844,
              719.8551635742188
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "2",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 7.019999980926514,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              474.2799987792969,
              175.20974731445312,
              477.7900085449219,
              182.229736328125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Atmospheric ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              477.82000732421875,
              176.52243041992188,
              542.7645263671875,
              187.50242614746094
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 88,
        "elements": []
      },
      {
        "page_number": 89,
        "elements": []
      },
      {
        "page_number": 90,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p90_b9.png",
            "bbox": [
              90.0,
              76.5,
              521.969970703125,
              333.04998779296875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 7-3. Development of a Thermal ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              344.81512451171875,
              229.5050048828125,
              354.8351135253906
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In this manner, convection transports heat from the Earth’s surface into the atmosphere. Because air is a ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              369.3624572753906,
              542.5977783203125,
              380.3424377441406
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "poor conductor of heat (see Table 5-3), convection is much more important than conduction as a heat ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              381.9624328613281,
              542.5667724609375,
              392.9424133300781
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "transport mechanism within the atmosphere. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              394.6224365234375,
              269.94500732421875,
              405.6024169921875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p90_b10.png",
            "bbox": [
              162.0,
              422.3600158691406,
              449.9800109863281,
              613.260009765625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 7-4. Example of Convection in the Atmosphere ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              624.6751098632812,
              292.80499267578125,
              634.6951293945312
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "7.3.2",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              649.4404907226562,
              97.98790740966797,
              662.46044921875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Latent Heat ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              108.0199966430664,
              649.4404907226562,
              172.2949981689453,
              662.46044921875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The phase transition of water and associated latent heat exchanges are largely responsible for transferring ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              670.1624145507812,
              542.3609008789062,
              681.1424560546875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the excess heat from the surface of the Earth into its atmosphere. As the Earth’s surface absorbs radiation, ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              682.8424072265625,
              542.7398071289062,
              693.8224487304688
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "some of the heat produced is used to evaporate (vaporize) water from oceans, lakes, rivers, soil, and ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              695.5023803710938,
              542.4684448242188,
              706.482421875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "vegetation. The water absorbs heat energy due to the latent heat of vaporization. Some of this water vapor ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              708.1624145507812,
              542.3499145507812,
              719.1424560546875
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 91,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "condenses to microscopic water droplets or deposits as ice crystals that are visible as clouds. During cloud ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              73.42244720458984,
              542.5036010742188,
              84.40245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "formation, the water vapor changes state, and latent heat is released into the atmosphere. During this ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              86.0824203491211,
              542.3719482421875,
              97.06242370605469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "process, the excess heat is transferred from the Earth’s surface into its atmosphere. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              98.7424545288086,
              438.3249816894531,
              109.72245788574219
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "7.4",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              123.58048248291016,
              88.2750015258789,
              136.60047912597656
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Heat Imbalance Variations with Latitude ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              100.80000305175781,
              123.58048248291016,
              316.3550109863281,
              136.60047912597656
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Global imbalances in radiational heating and cooling occur not only vertically between the Earth’s surface ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              144.30245971679688,
              542.7631225585938,
              155.28245544433594
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "and its atmosphere but also horizontally with latitude. Since the Earth is essentially spherical, parallel beams ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              156.96243286132812,
              542.5037231445312,
              167.9424285888672
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "of incoming solar radiation strike lower latitudes more directly than higher latitudes (see Figure 7-5); that ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              169.62246704101562,
              542.8050537109375,
              180.6024627685547
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "is, the solar zenith angle is lower, and the Sun is more directly overhead in equatorial regions than at the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              182.28244018554688,
              542.5164184570312,
              193.26243591308594
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "poles. At higher latitudes, solar radiation is spread over a larger area and is less intense per unit surface area ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              194.94247436523438,
              542.6461181640625,
              205.92247009277344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "than at lower latitudes. Thus, the Earth absorbs more solar radiation at lower latitudes than higher latitudes, ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              207.54244995117188,
              542.547607421875,
              218.52244567871094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "which creates heat imbalances and temperature gradients between the Equator and the poles. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              220.20242309570312,
              481.94500732421875,
              231.1824188232422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p91_b9.png",
            "bbox": [
              90.0,
              247.94000244140625,
              521.969970703125,
              572.4400024414062
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 7-5. Solar Zenith Angle Variations with Latitude ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              583.755126953125,
              298.385009765625,
              593.775146484375
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The emission of terrestrial radiation also varies by latitude, but less so than the absorption of solar radiation. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              608.3024291992188,
              542.5913696289062,
              619.282470703125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Terrestrial radiation emission decreases with increasing latitude due to a drop in temperature with latitude. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              620.96240234375,
              542.7714233398438,
              631.9424438476562
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Thus, at higher latitudes, the annual rate of cooling exceeds the rate of warming, while the reverse is true ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              633.6224365234375,
              542.3062133789062,
              644.6024780273438
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "at lower latitudes. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              646.2824096679688,
              153.2449951171875,
              657.262451171875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Averaged over all latitudes, incoming solar radiation must equal outgoing terrestrial radiation. Otherwise, ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              664.8823852539062,
              542.4816284179688,
              675.8624267578125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the Earth would be unable to maintain a constant average temperature. About 35° latitude in both ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              677.5424194335938,
              542.4159545898438,
              688.5224609375
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "hemispheres is where incoming and outgoing radiation is equal. This implies there is annual net cooling at ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              690.222412109375,
              542.604248046875,
              701.2024536132812
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "higher latitudes and net warming at lower latitudes; however, this is untrue. The excess heat in the tropics ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              702.8823852539062,
              542.514892578125,
              713.8624267578125
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 92,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "must be transported polar by some mechanism(s). This poleward heat transport is accomplished by ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              73.42244720458984,
              542.7080688476562,
              84.40245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "atmospheric circulations, weather, and ocean currents. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              86.0824203491211,
              313.625,
              97.06242370605469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "7.5",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              110.96048736572266,
              88.2750015258789,
              123.9804916381836
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Seasons ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              100.80000305175781,
              110.96048736572266,
              145.9550018310547,
              123.9804916381836
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Seasons are caused by the tilt of the Earth’s rotational axis as the Earth orbits the Sun (see Figure 7-6). The ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              131.70242309570312,
              542.79541015625,
              142.6824188232422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Earth’s rotational axis is tilted by 23½° from the perpendicular drawn to the plane of the Earth’s orbit about ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              144.30245971679688,
              542.7589721679688,
              155.28245544433594
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the Sun and points the same direction in space all year long. The North Pole is tilted most directly toward ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              156.96243286132812,
              542.5669555664062,
              167.9424285888672
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the Sun during the summer solstice. Thus, in the Northern Hemisphere, the longest day of the year (lowest ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              169.62246704101562,
              542.58740234375,
              180.6024627685547
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "solar zenith angle) occurs on the summer solstice (approximately June 22), while the shortest day of the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              182.28244018554688,
              542.66796875,
              193.26243591308594
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "year (highest solar zenith angle) occurs on the winter solstice (approximately December 22). Day and night ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              194.94247436523438,
              542.7590942382812,
              205.92247009277344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "are of equal length (12 hours) worldwide on the vernal equinox (approximately March 21) and the autumnal ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              207.54244995117188,
              542.7554321289062,
              218.52244567871094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "equinox (approximately September 23). ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              220.20242309570312,
              250.08499145507812,
              231.1824188232422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p92_b8.png",
            "bbox": [
              90.0,
              247.94003295898438,
              521.9299926757812,
              519.2900390625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 7-6. Solar Zenith Angle Variations with Northern Hemisphere Seasons ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              531.255126953125,
              386.7250061035156,
              541.275146484375
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 7-7 illustrates the average seasonal temperature variation in the Northern Hemisphere. Note that the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              555.8024291992188,
              542.7730712890625,
              566.782470703125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "warmest (coldest days) of the year occur after the summer (winter) solstice. This is due to the time lag ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              568.46240234375,
              542.3959350585938,
              579.4424438476562
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "necessary for heat flow processes to fully heat (cool) the surface of the Earth. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              581.1224365234375,
              414.9849853515625,
              592.1024780273438
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 93,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p93_b8.png",
            "bbox": [
              108.0,
              76.5,
              503.5,
              285.8800048828125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 7-7. Average Seasonal Temperature Variation in the Northern Hemisphere ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              298.31512451171875,
              401.6650085449219,
              308.3351135253906
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "7.6",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              323.0804748535156,
              88.2750015258789,
              336.1004943847656
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Diurnal Temperature Variation ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              100.80000305175781,
              323.0804748535156,
              265.7149963378906,
              336.1004943847656
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Diurnal temperature variation is the daytime maximum and nighttime minimum of air temperature due to ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              343.8024597167969,
              542.4379272460938,
              354.7824401855469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "variations of insolation caused by the rising and setting of the Sun (i.e., variations of solar zenith angle) as ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              356.4624328613281,
              542.6660766601562,
              367.4424133300781
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the Earth rotates around its axis. Figure 7-8 depicts the typical diurnal temperature and radiation variations ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              369.06243896484375,
              542.6654663085938,
              380.04241943359375
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "over land when the sky is clear. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              381.7224426269531,
              213.72499084472656,
              392.7024230957031
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p93_b9.png",
            "bbox": [
              117.0,
              409.4540100097656,
              494.7200012207031,
              693.8040161132812
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 7-8. Clear Sky Diurnal Temperature and Radiation Variations Over Land ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              706.2951049804688,
              395.7850036621094,
              716.3151245117188
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 94,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Warming and cooling of the Earth depend on an imbalance between solar and terrestrial radiation. The ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              73.42244720458984,
              542.3828735351562,
              84.40245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Earth receives heat during the day through incoming solar radiation. It loses heat to space by outgoing ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              86.0824203491211,
              542.6435546875,
              97.06242370605469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "terrestrial radiation both day and night. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              98.7424545288086,
              246.42498779296875,
              109.72245788574219
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Shortly after sunrise, incoming solar radiation received at the Earth’s surface (insolation) becomes greater ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              117.3424301147461,
              542.6321411132812,
              128.3224334716797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "than outgoing terrestrial radiation and the Earth’s surface warms. Peak insolation occurs around noon, but ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              130.02243041992188,
              542.66552734375,
              141.00242614746094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "maximum surface air temperature usually occurs during the midafternoon. This lag is necessary for the air ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              142.68246459960938,
              542.4158935546875,
              153.66246032714844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "near the ground to heat up due to conduction and convection with the surface. The Earth begins to cool ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              155.34243774414062,
              542.5071411132812,
              166.3224334716797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "once the rate of outgoing terrestrial radiation exceeds the rate of insolation. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              168.00247192382812,
              405.2049865722656,
              178.9824676513672
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "At night, insolation is absent but outgoing terrestrial radiation continues, and the Earth’s surface continues ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              186.60244750976562,
              542.7364501953125,
              197.5824432373047
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "to cool. Cooling continues until shortly after sunrise, when incoming solar radiation once again exceeds ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              199.26242065429688,
              542.4049682617188,
              210.24241638183594
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "outgoing terrestrial radiation. Minimum surface air temperature usually occurs shortly after sunrise. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              211.92245483398438,
              513.0850219726562,
              222.90245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The magnitude of diurnal temperature variation is primarily influenced by surface type, latitude, sky cover ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              230.58242797851562,
              542.7137451171875,
              241.5624237060547
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "(e.g., clouds or pollutants), water vapor content of the air, and wind speed. Temperature variation is ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              243.24246215820312,
              542.4102783203125,
              254.2224578857422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "maximized over land, at low latitudes, with a clear sky, dry air, and light wind. Conversely, temperature ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              255.84243774414062,
              542.3938598632812,
              266.8224182128906
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "variation is minimized over water, at high latitudes, with a cloudy sky, moist air, and strong wind. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              268.5024719238281,
              505.8249816894531,
              279.4824523925781
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
